## 3. Interactive Debugging and Refinement

The first solution generated by an AI assistant may not always be correct.

AI-generated code can sometimes:

- Use outdated APIs
- Generate incorrect parameters
- Misunderstand library behavior
- Produce incorrect data types
- Generate invalid function names
- Miss required imports
- Make incorrect assumptions
- Produce syntactically valid but logically incorrect code

For this reason, AI-assisted development should be an **iterative process**.

---

### Recommended Debugging Workflow

A practical debugging process is:

```text
Generate
   ↓
Execute
   ↓
Inspect Error
   ↓
Read Traceback
   ↓
Send Error to AI
   ↓
Ask for Explanation
   ↓
Review Solution
   ↓
Refine Code
   ↓
Execute Again
   ↓
Validate
```

One of the most useful techniques is to provide the **actual traceback** to the AI assistant.

Instead of simply saying:

```text
My code doesn't work.
```

a better prompt would be:

```text
Here is my Python code.

Here is the complete traceback.

Explain why the error occurs and show me how to correct it.

Do not change unrelated parts of the program.
```

Providing the actual error gives the AI much more information about the problem.

