## Exercise 2 — AI-Assisted Debugging

The following example demonstrates how AI can assist with debugging.

```python
image = np.zeros((100, 100, 3), dtype=np.uint8)

cv2.rectangle(
    image,
    (10, 10),
    (50, 50),
    (255, 0, 0),
    2
)
```

When experimenting with different argument types or values, OpenCV may generate errors if the provided parameters do not match what the function expects.

---

### Example Debugging Prompt

> Explain the error produced by `cv2.rectangle`, identify which argument is incorrect, and show me the Pythonic way to correct it.

A stronger debugging prompt could be:

```text
The following OpenCV code produces an error.

Explain what the error means.

Identify which argument is incorrect.

Show me the Pythonic way to fix the problem.

Do not simply provide the corrected code.
Explain why the correction works.
```

This last instruction is particularly important.

The goal is not simply to obtain working code.

The goal is to **understand why the code works**.

When working with AI-generated solutions, always verify that the explanation matches the actual behavior of the program.

